#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306158 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4abe11e-febf-5461-96ee-bca8a7713bdf" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Linear Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c8afb12-c143-5d6c-9273-ac8abd85f79d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306160} {:answer/id #uuid "65edb11e-1e0e-5d4b-bc4d-5dbf5cafa493" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306161} {:answer/id #uuid "9c2c7d95-276c-51d5-8e6e-1785b9a65df0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306162} {:answer/id #uuid "d68b4eca-f9a8-5f56-a553-5d20378e9e19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306163} {:answer/id #uuid "213a9033-3c23-597e-a74b-6bf809abfba0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306164}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c8afb12-c143-5d6c-9273-ac8abd85f79d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306160} :answer-field/id #uuid "98cde449-95b7-5409-84ce-9958ec24578e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306159}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "042f12f3-e4d4-5dee-9b70-77f357143515" :question/math-academy-id "q-117680" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/assets/a433e9e9f2018c1f964f13b0ef248f72253f99f071f44e029c928598e9d04a32.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Find the value of ${\\int }_{-4}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of two parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-4,0)$ and the line segment for $x\\in [0,2].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={{{\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,0).$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [0,2].$ The corresponding region is a square, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={s}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =8+4 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306198 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb30dbbf-4f83-59d0-b712-f0e592aafb27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Curved Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17280961-b8ed-5aa6-81f7-d8b50b1a9829" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11+\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306200} {:answer/id #uuid "5c96d804-f537-52e4-b34d-fd4d435b98cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306201} {:answer/id #uuid "80b4f644-df23-51c0-8831-2901cfcbe042" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2+3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306202} {:answer/id #uuid "ccc19608-29e4-5590-8c3a-58a595a50ab1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8-\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306203} {:answer/id #uuid "7e76dd41-e945-5009-a407-63b8f134dd5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7+2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c96d804-f537-52e4-b34d-fd4d435b98cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306201} :answer-field/id #uuid "f394f966-6c73-55ab-af28-cd289c2d467d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306199}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ef200c23-7d18-56c9-a45a-07e8091b06fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-49215" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/assets/11cf2a28b6ce6281de0299a4a585bc3c98bf891f6d958401f22aab2fc6ce5ef2.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of ${\\int }_{-4}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of three parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-4,-2],$ the arc of a semi-circle for $x\\in (-2,2],$ and the line segment for $x\\in (2,3].$  So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-4}^{-2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThe first integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,-2].$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =bh \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe second integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (-2,2].$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle of radius $2.$ Thus, we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-2\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe third integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (2,3].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid. Thus, we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2+4)(1) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2}+{A}_{3} \\\\  & =6-2\\pi +3 \\\\  & =9-2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
